{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgsrDgFynZWedTjvTyEFG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every run was dying at startup because of the Keychain lookup, not the OpenRouter key. I fixed it in `run-vibe.sh` and wrote the cause to `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Cause:** Vibe's built-in `mistral` provider is still in its provider list even though the active model uses OpenRouter. At startup it looks up `MISTRAL_API_KEY` in the macOS Keychain by running `/usr/bin/security`. The sandbox profile `agent.sb` denies that exec, so it fails with `EPERM`. Vibe doesn't catch that error, so the process exits with status 2 before any model request. I reproduced this inside the same sandbox profile.\n\n**Fix:** `run-vibe.sh` now passes `VIBE_TEST_DISABLE_KEYRING=1` into the sandboxed environment. Vibe then skips every Keychain read. I left `agent.sb` alone rather than allowing `/usr/bin/security`, since that would let the agent reach the Keychain client.\n\n**Verification:** I re-ran the script with a dummy key. It now gets past startup and fails only at OpenRouter's auth check, as expected for an invalid key. I did not make a real model call, so a run with your real key is still untested. I restored the tracked `logs/last-run.*` files to the original failure logs afterwards.\n\n**Caveat:** The variable name says \"TEST\", but Vibe reads it in production code. If a future Vibe release renames it, the Keychain error will come back. The reproduction in `ANSWER.md` will show it.\n\nChanges are uncommitted: `run-vibe.sh` modified, `ANSWER.md` added."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1429,"cache_read_input_tokens":45910,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1429},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics"